{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ab09651bf2b85de9","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is an on-premises network?**|What is an on-premises network?]]","x":-100,"y":8725,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"84cfc32d7c0c51cc","type":"text","text":"An `on-premises network` is a company's own physical network outside the cloud.  \nFor example, your company may have:\n```\nOffice building\n├── Routers\n├── Switches\n├── Servers\n└── Computers\n```\nThis network is managed by the company, not by AWS.  ","x":300,"y":8725,"width":500,"height":310},
		{"id":"03ca7f8ffb859a9b","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a flat network?**|What is a flat network?]]","x":-100,"y":9080,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"22c13d0eba112043","type":"text","text":"A `flat network` is a simple network without strong separation between different parts.  \nIn a flat network, many resources may exist in the same network area.  \nThis gives less control compared to a properly designed VPC with subnets and routing.  ","x":300,"y":9080,"width":500,"height":175},
		{"id":"b6bdc083585af571","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a shared network?**|What is a shared network?]]","x":-100,"y":9260,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"113290eee713d890","type":"text","text":"A `shared network` is a network used by many customers or systems together.  \nEC2-Classic used a shared network model in the past.  \nVPC is better because it gives each customer isolated network control.  ","x":300,"y":9260,"width":500,"height":145},
		{"id":"c6b4345e91000184","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is network architecture?**|What is network architecture?]]","x":-100,"y":9440,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"12e291f4ff2e3a52","type":"text","text":"`Network architecture` is the overall design of how your network is organized.  \nIt includes:\n```\nVPCs\nSubnets\nRoute tables\nGateways\nSecurity groups\nCIDR blocks\nTraffic flow\n```\nGood network architecture makes your system more secure and easier to manage.  ","x":300,"y":9440,"width":500,"height":378},
		{"id":"13c4611e1ae59254","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is Network Address Translation?**|What is Network Address Translation?]]","x":-100,"y":9860,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d0495e87c04b3621","type":"text","text":"`Network Address Translation`, or `NAT`, means changing one IP address into another IP address when traffic moves between networks.  \nFor example, a private EC2 instance may use a NAT Gateway to access the internet.  \nThe internet sees the NAT Gateway's public IP address, not the private EC2 instance's private IP address.  ","x":300,"y":9860,"width":500,"height":202}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"a8d5e891f243992b","fromNode":"ab09651bf2b85de9","fromSide":"right","toNode":"84cfc32d7c0c51cc","toSide":"left"},
